FILE: app/src/center-on.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function centerOnLatLon(camera: GeospatialCamera, latitudeDeg: number, longitudeDeg: number): void {
    const R = camera.limits.planetRadius;
    const lat = (latitudeDeg * Math.PI) / 180;
    const lon = (longitudeDeg * Math.PI) / 180;

    const x = R * Math.cos(lat) * Math.cos(lon);
    const y = -R * Math.cos(lat) * Math.sin(lon);
    const z = R * Math.sin(lat);

    camera.center = { x, y, z };
}
```

I wrote `src/center-on.ts` which computes the 3D position on the planet sphere using the axis convention from the notes (north = +Z, the lat-0/lon-0 point on +X, east = −Y) and assigns a fresh object to `camera.center` so the camera re-orbits immediately while keeping its yaw, pitch, and radius unchanged. I'm confident in the coordinate mapping (verified against note 54 and the requirement that yaw 90° at the origin-centre looks toward positive longitude); the only thing I can't verify without the library itself is whether `Vec3` has any extra fields beyond `x`, `y`, `z` that a plain object literal would omit.
